```

Bootstrap using `.agents/skills/harness-bootstrap/SKILL.md`. Claude users can invoke
`/harness-bootstrap`; the skill files in `.claude/skills` share the canonical copies.
Adopt in order: AGENTS.md → gates → hooks → invariant tests → golden → evals →
reviewer → plans/loop. Add each layer only when its predecessor provides value.

Use `scripts/agent.sh <enabled-agent>` for interactive work. Hooks load at session
startup: restart after changing them. The bootstrap must configure review rules
before the loop can complete work. Copy docs/plans/template to a named plan, fill
its queue (schema next to it), then try:

```sh
scripts/run_units.sh --agent {{ agents_enabled[0] if agents_enabled else 'codex' }} --plan docs/plans/template --dry-run
```

The loop works on a clean committed checkout, locks the Git common directory,
runs one fresh child per unit, checks scope, labels and goldens, runs gates and a
fresh reviewer, then stages explicit paths and commits locally. It never pushes.
Failures preserve work for inspection. The model prompt is not a security boundary;
Codex workspace sandbox does not guarantee Git denial on every CLI version.
Run in a disposable checkout and inspect CLI sandbox support before unattended use.
No agents are required for check.sh or offline tests.

## Pitfalls checklist

- Codex Stop stdout must contain JSON only; gate output goes to a log. Return
  `{}` on success; block once on failure. When `stop_hook_active` is true, warn
  and end instead of triggering an infinite continuation. A warning is not a pass.
- Restart the agent after hook/config changes; an existing session may retain old hooks.
- Test agent launch plus an actual shell/test command: nested OS sandboxes may fail.
  Never solve this by disabling the sandbox or adding bypass flags.
- tmux servers keep old environments. Refresh intended variables explicitly and
  verify login from the pane; do not print secrets or assume a new shell refreshed it.
- Set the dotenv opt-out before application imports; isolate real config with a
  temporary fixture. Socket blocking alone cannot prevent local secret reads.
- The loop owns Git. Children leave changes unstaged/uncommitted; inspect failures
  before retrying. Use the project venv and PYTHONPATH for alternate checkouts.
- Accepted goldens are evidence, not files to refresh until tests pass. Mutation-test
  important guards. Keep stable ordering and clocks; mock external seams only.
- Record rejected/not-adopted approaches with evidence, limits and revisit criteria.
  Read ADRs before retrying; tiny eval sets don't justify broad conclusions. Keep
  labels owner-controlled and compare metric values, not just a scorer's exit code.

The dotenv/strict-xfail examples illustrate patterns, not adopted product rules.
The label hook is a local guard; branch protection and owner review enforce policy.
